<title>BELGIUM: Anhyp brings action over bad loans.</title>
<headline>Anhyp brings action over bad loans.</headline>
<dateline>ANTWERP, Belgium 1997-04-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>Anhyp, a small listed Belgian mortgage and savings bank, said on Monday it had taken legal action against unknown parties over bad property loans which sparked its fall into the red in 1995.</p>
<p>&quot;Where Anhyp feels it has been disadvantaged it has decided to lodge a complaint against people unknown,&quot; chief executive officer Carl Holsters told the annual shareholders meeting.</p>
<p>He gave no details.</p>
<p>Anhyp, hammered by losses on bad loans in the real estate sector in France and Belgium, narrowed its consolidated net loss to 428 million Belgian francs in 1996 from 1.5 billion loss in 1995. It made 1.06 billion profit in 1994.</p>
<p>Holsters said he hoped the bank would be able to make non-consolidated profit of about one billion in 1997 after a 467 million franc loss in 1996.</p>
<p>Shareholders overwhelmingly approved the 1996 accounts.</p>
